Logging in the framework is achieved through the usage of predetermined log
streams. There are streams available for debugging, system logs, user
communication, error messages and fatal error messages.  These classes have the
same interface as `std::cout`, with the difference that they do not print to
stdout, but instead automatically publish their messages to the framework. They
publish as soon as they are passed `std::endl`, or when their buffer is full.
See the class documentation for information on how to set the buffer size.

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_LOG_MAIN_CPP@
```

The log can be viewed in real-time by calling

```txt
$ dls -llog
```

For this example program you won't be able to see your logs as they will be
drowned out by the noise of other components. To filter out what you want to
see, simply use standard Unix tools. For instance, the following commands may be
useful:

```txt
$ dls -llog | grep example_logging
$ dls -llog | grep INFO
$ dls -llog | grep DEBUG | grep example_logging
```
